    {
      "turn": 2,
      "doctor_question": "Have you always struggled with this kind of focus, or is this something that has only started happening more recently?",
      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_1",
      "patient_response": "Always? Yeah, definitely always. Even back when I was a kid, like way before middle school, I was exactly like this. My teachers were constantly moving my seat because I just couldn't stay put. I'd be up and down, calling out the answers without raising my hand, always getting in trouble for butting into what the other kids were working on. \n\nI remember my third-grade teacher, Mrs. Gable. She had this huge wooden desk up front. They really don't make furniture like that anymore, you know? Solid oak. My uncle actually does woodworking on the side, makes these beautiful coffee tables and cabinets. He tried to teach me how to do it once, but you gotta wait so long for the wood glue to dry and the varnish to set, and I just can't stand sitting around waiting for paint to dry, literally. I'd rather be doing something with my hands that moves fast, which is why I like the production line, usually, when the machines actually work. \n\nBut anyway, yeah, Mrs. Gable moved my desk right next to hers, right up against the chalkboard. The chalk dust used to get all over my clothes. My mom hated that. She was always scrubbing at my jeans. Speaking of which, the new uniforms at the plant pick up grease like you wouldn't believe. I don't know what kind of fabric they switched to, but it's terrible.",
